ALVA — Discover and roster UI refinement

Objective

Improve the visual consistency, readability, typography, and spacing of the ALVA game’s Discover interface and fighter roster previews.

Repository: https://github.com/hiyroscript/alva

First, inspect the existing implementation and understand how Discover, fighter selection, character previews, localization, and navigation work together. Then implement the following changes directly in the existing architecture.

This is a focused UI/UX refinement, not a redesign. Preserve ALVA’s current visual identity, colors, typography, layout structure, game mechanics, and navigation system.

1. Lower the “Read play style” action when there is no Confirm button

Current behavior

The game uses a shared FighterRoster component across several screens, including fighter selection and Discover.

The preview information area displays:

* Difficulty stars or a status badge.
* The fighter’s name.
* The “Read play style” action.
* A Confirm button, when appropriate.

Discover uses FighterBrowser, which extends FighterRoster but intentionally does not display a Confirm button.

Currently, the “Read play style” action can appear too close to the fighter name and difficulty stars when no Confirm button is present.

Required changes

For any roster preview that does not have a Confirm button:

1. Position “Read play style” lower within the preview information area.
2. Keep the action aligned to the bottom-right.
3. Create a more noticeable but natural vertical separation between the fighter name/difficulty information and the action.
4. Preserve the difficulty stars above the fighter name.
5. Maintain the current underlined text-button appearance rather than turning it into a large primary button.
6. Preserve keyboard, gamepad, mouse, and touch accessibility.
7. Keep its existing functionality: clicking it must still open the correct fighter’s play-style dialog.
8. Do not introduce an empty Confirm button or a placeholder occupying its position.

Use the absence of the Confirm button as the condition for this layout variation. Prefer a reusable, scoped class or equivalent component state rather than styling based on individual fighter IDs.

The normal roster layout, where a Confirm button exists, must remain unchanged.

The spacing must also adapt to short landscape windows and mobile devices without clipping text or sacrificing excessive space from the animated fighter preview.

Relevant files

* js/ui/fighter-roster.js
* js/ui/fighter-browser.js
* css/setup.css
* css/discover.css

2. Completely remove the Passives section from Discover

Current behavior

Discover currently contains four sections:

1. Fighters
2. Movement
3. Launch
4. Passives

Passives is registered as an intentionally empty section.

Required changes

Completely remove Passives from the Discover interface.

The final navigation must have exactly three sections, in this order:

Fighters → Movement → Launch

Requirements:

* Remove Passives from the section registration in js/screens/discover-screen.js.
* Remove its tab and associated empty panel.
* Remove obsolete Passives-specific Discover scaffolding and comments.
* Remove unused localization entries when appropriate.
* Update any documentation that describes Discover as having four sections.
* Preserve the existing behavior where opening Discover always selects Fighters first.
* Ensure clicking, keyboard navigation, and gamepad navigation work normally with three tabs.
* Ensure the horizontal mobile navigation naturally accommodates the remaining three tabs.
* Preserve correct ARIA relationships, selected states, and focus handling.

Important: Removing Passives from Discover does not mean removing passive abilities or related mechanics from the fighting game. This change affects only the Discover interface.

3. Redesign the Movement information as flowing paragraphs

Current behavior

The Movement section contains an introductory explanation about universal movement, followed by several individual entries presented inside bordered rectangular holders.

The current movement topics come from MOVEMENT_GUIDE in js/data/movement.js:

* Run
* Jump
* Triple jump
* Fast fall
* Dash
* Air dash

These entries currently use .discover-tiers, .discover-tier, and decorative movement markers.

Required changes

Remove the individual holder/card presentation for Movement.

Instead, display the information as normal readable text immediately after the introductory paragraph.

The desired hierarchy is:

Movement

Universal movement

[Existing introductory paragraph explaining that movement is universal.]

[One additional flowing paragraph explaining Run, Jump, Triple jump, Fast fall, Dash, and Air dash.]

Formatting

1. Keep the original introductory paragraph.
2. Add a second paragraph immediately below it.
3. Combine the six movement explanations into one coherent paragraph.
4. Preserve the information provided by the existing movement registry.
5. Use subtle inline emphasis, such as bold movement names, if it improves readability.
6. Do not use cards, outlined holders, shaded blocks, decorative bullet markers, or tier-style containers.
7. Use natural punctuation and spacing between explanations.
8. Maintain the same readable typography, line height, and muted text palette used in Discover.
9. Let the paragraph occupy a sensible reading width rather than forcing the text into unnecessarily narrow columns.
10. Ensure the paragraph wraps naturally and remains readable on desktop and mobile.

Implementation constraints

Continue deriving the content from MOVEMENT_GUIDE and its localized translation keys.

Do not duplicate all movement descriptions as a separate hardcoded English paragraph that could drift away from the game’s source data.

Update buildMovementPage() in js/screens/discover-screen.js to render the content using appropriate semantic paragraph elements.

Remove movement-only visual styles that are no longer needed, without breaking the Launch section’s list presentation.

Keep the actual universal movement mechanics and tuning values untouched.

Relevant files

* js/screens/discover-screen.js
* css/discover.css
* js/data/movement.js — reference data, not a request to change gameplay
* js/localization/strings/en.js
* js/localization/strings/fr.js

4. Correct capitalization and grammar throughout Discover

Current problem

Many labels are displayed entirely in uppercase because of CSS text-transform: uppercase.

Examples include:

* MOVEMENT
* LAUNCH
* UNIVERSAL MOVEMENT
* Movement entry names and Launch headings

This makes the interface less natural to read.

Required changes

Use normal, grammatically appropriate capitalization throughout the entire Discover interface.

Examples:

Current appearance	Desired appearance
FIGHTERS	Fighters
MOVEMENT	Movement
LAUNCH	Launch
UNIVERSAL MOVEMENT	Universal movement
RUN	Run
JUMP	Jump
TRIPLE JUMP	Triple jump
FAST FALL	Fast fall
AIR DASH	Air dash
ROSTER	Roster
LOCKED	Locked
NOT RATED	Not rated

Review the entire Discover interface, not only the main navigation.

This includes:

* Discover navigation tabs.
* Page headings.
* Section headings.
* Movement descriptions and inline names.
* Launch section headings.
* Launch value and direction labels.
* Roster panel headings.
* Fighter preview labels and statuses.
* The “Read play style” action.
* The play-style dialog’s headings and descriptions.

Capitalization rules

Use natural sentence case for regular headings and labels.

Keep proper names, official mechanic names, acronyms, and technical terms correctly capitalized when required by the game’s established terminology.

For example, preserve the meaningful capitalization of established terms such as “Launch Point”, “Base Launch”, and “Directional Launch” when they refer to the game’s named mechanics.

Do not blindly convert all text to lowercase. Do not change the ALVA brand name.

Correct actual spelling, grammar, and punctuation problems where necessary, without altering the meaning of gameplay descriptions.

CSS requirements

Inspect and correct uppercase transformations within these selectors:

* .discover-tab
* .discover-page-title
* .discover-entry-title
* .discover-tier-name

Also inspect shared styles such as .panel-title and .status-badge, which can affect text inside Discover.

Use Discover-scoped overrides where needed so capitalization changes do not unintentionally alter the appearance of other game screens.

Preserve the existing font hierarchy, weight, color, and overall styling as much as possible.

Localization

ALVA supports English and French. All modifications must respect both languages.

Review the relevant strings in:

* js/localization/strings/en.js
* js/localization/strings/fr.js

Ensure capitalization and grammar are appropriate for each language independently.

Do not apply English capitalization conventions blindly to French.

Preserve localization keys that remain in use and ensure language switching continues to update every relevant UI element.

5. Preserve the established architecture

ALVA is built using vanilla JavaScript, CSS, HTML, and Canvas 2D. It does not require a frontend framework or build system.

Follow the architecture already established by the repository.

Important constraints:

* Do not introduce React, Vue, or other new frameworks.
* Do not alter combat systems, player movement, fighter definitions, difficulty values, or launch calculations.
* Do not modify character art or animation assets.
* Do not introduce fighter-ID-specific special cases in shared UI components.
* Do not modify the protected character_rule file.
* Preserve the intentional internal codename spelling mouvment where required by codename_rule. This spelling is part of the game’s internal naming contract and is not a player-facing typo to correct.
* Preserve all existing navigation, focus management, and modal functionality.
* Keep the implementation concise and consistent with existing code conventions.

6. Responsive design and accessibility

Verify that the modified interface works in:

* Desktop landscape.
* Short laptop-sized landscape windows.
* Mobile landscape.
* Narrow screens where Discover uses horizontal navigation.
* English and French.

Ensure:

* No clipped headings or overflowing paragraphs.
* No horizontal scrolling caused by Movement descriptions.
* Readable spacing between the introductory and detailed Movement paragraphs.
* Proper placement of “Read play style” in previews without Confirm.
* Consistent difficulty-star alignment.
* Correct focus indicators and keyboard navigation.
* Accessible tab labels, tab-panel relationships, and localized names.
* No invisible Passives tab or panel remaining in the navigation.
* No regressions in the Launch reference layout.

7. Update automated tests

The repository uses Node’s built-in test runner.

Review and update tests/interface/discover.test.mjs, along with other affected tests.

Specifically:

1. Change the expected Discover tab count from four to three.
2. Remove assertions expecting Passives to exist.
3. Update keyboard/gamepad navigation tests for the three-tab order.
4. Update Movement tests to expect introductory and detailed paragraphs rather than a list of bordered rows.
5. Verify all six movement descriptions are still present and correctly localized.
6. Add or update coverage for normal-case Discover labels.
7. Verify the play-style action is positioned using the no-Confirm layout variant.
8. Verify Confirm-enabled rosters retain their existing structure and behavior.
9. Preserve existing tests for difficulty ratings, locked fighters, unrated fighters, modal interactions, and roster selection.
10. Verify both English and French UI strings.

Run:

node --test

Correct all failures caused by the intended changes. Do not delete meaningful test coverage merely to make the suite pass.

Automated tests do not fully verify browser layout, so also inspect the responsive visual behavior when a browser environment is available.

8. Final acceptance criteria

The task is complete only when:

* [ ]	“Read play style” sits noticeably lower, toward the bottom-right of the preview information area, whenever the roster has no Confirm button.
* [ ]	Rosters that do have a Confirm button remain visually unchanged.
* [ ]	Discover has exactly three tabs: Fighters, Movement, Launch.
* [ ]	Passives is no longer visible or registered as a Discover section.
* [ ]	Movement’s six explanations appear in one readable paragraph directly after the introductory paragraph.
* [ ]	Movement no longer uses individual outlined holders.
* [ ]	Launch retains its existing mechanic explanations and structured reference content.
* [ ]	Discover text uses natural capitalization rather than forced all-caps styling.
* [ ]	English and French remain correct and functional.
* [ ]	Keyboard, gamepad, mouse, and touch interactions remain functional.
* [ ]	The relevant tests are updated and the full suite passes.
* [ ]	No unrelated gameplay mechanics or visuals are modified.

9. Final deliverable

Implement the modifications in the repository rather than stopping at a proposed plan.

After implementation, provide:

1. A concise summary of the changes.
2. The actual files modified.
3. An explanation of how the no-Confirm roster layout works.
4. Confirmation that the three-tab Discover navigation functions correctly.
5. The automated test results.
6. Any remaining visual verification limitations.

Priority: Deliver a cleaner, more polished, naturally readable Discover interface while preserving the existing ALVA design language and gameplay behavior. Avoid unnecessary refactoring or unrelated changes.
